  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWell above normal temperatures will continue across the region\nthrough Thursday, though it will not be as hot as the last \ncouple of days. Highs today will be in the 80s, with readings \nThursday in the middle 80s to 90 degrees. \n\nModels show a frontal boundary, currently extending from \nnorthern Indiana into northern Missouri, sinking south and \nlaying out from southwest to northeast across the PAH forecast \narea (fa) by this evening. Showers and some thunderstorms will \naccompany this boundary, with convection spreading southeast \nacross the PAH fa from 09z-18z. Chances will peak at midday at \n70-90%, gradually decreasing from west to east in the afternoon.\nChances overnight will decrease a little more with loss of \nheating, ranging from 15-30% south to 40- 50% north. On \nThursday, a surface low moving through Missouri will lift the \nboundary north, and convection will be focused along and north \nof the boundary. Precipitation chances will be 30-60% mainly \nacross our northern half of counties. By Thursday night, the low\nwill be dragging a cold front across the PAH fa. The model \nblend produces 30-60% chances across most of our region, but \nkeeps most of west Kentucky and extreme southeast Missouri dry \nwith its passage. Rainfall totals will generally be from a\nquarter inch to around an inch, with localized higher amounts.\n\nSPC includes our entire area in a Marginal Risk of severe\nthunderstorms, with the best potential this afternoon. On\nThursday during the late afternoon and evening, there a a \nMarginal Risk of severe storms north of the boundary, mainly\nalong and north of a line from Du Quoin Illinois to Carmi to \nWinslow Indiana. The main concern with storms will be damaging \nwind gusts. \n\nBehind the front, it will become quite breezy Friday with north\nto northwest winds at 15-25mph and gusts near 30mph. These winds\nwill help usher in much cooler air, with highs Friday in the\n60s, and Saturday in the 60 to 65 degree range. With calm winds\nSaturday, we will see our chilliest temperatures of the season \nin the 35 to 40 degree range, and patchy frost is not out of the\nquestion.\n\nOn Sunday, a return of southerly winds will begin a warming \ntrend, with temperatures near normal by Monday, and a little \nabove normal by Tuesday.",
